Relevant columns and how they will be used  
`primary_type`: filter to only “MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT” incidents  
`year`: quick filter to only the 2016 rows (cheaper than filtering on the TIMESTAMP directly)  
`date`: extract the calendar month so we can aggregate incidents by month  
`unique_key`: a one‑to‑one identifier for each crime record – COUNT(unique_key) → number of incidents  

Typical conditions that will be used  
• `WHERE year = 2016`  
• `AND primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'`  
• `GROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM date)` (or an equivalent month expression)  

Below are ten progressively richer BigQuery SQL snippets, each capped with `LIMIT 20` (or fewer), culminating in the query that will return the highest single‑month count of motor‑vehicle thefts that occurred in 2016.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at raw 2016 crime rows (any crime type)
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
WHERE year = 2016
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. See the distinct primary crime types that exist in 2016
SELECT DISTINCT primary_type
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
WHERE year = 2016
ORDER BY primary_type
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Count each primary crime type in 2016
SELECT primary_type, COUNT(unique_key) AS incidents
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
WHERE year = 2016
GROUP BY primary_type
ORDER BY incidents DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Return a few rows for 2016 motor‑vehicle thefts only
SELECT unique_key, date, block, primary_type
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
WHERE year = 2016
  AND primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Add the month number so we can see which month each sample row came from
SELECT unique_key,
       EXTRACT(MONTH FROM date) AS month_num,
       date,
       block
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
WHERE year = 2016
  AND primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Aggregate motor‑vehicle thefts by month of 2016
SELECT EXTRACT(MONTH FROM date) AS month_num,
       COUNT(unique_key)           AS mv_thefts
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
WHERE year = 2016
  AND primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
GROUP BY month_num
ORDER BY month_num
LIMIT 20;     -- returns at most 12 rows (one for each month)
```

```sql
-- 7. Same as #6 but ordered by descending count to see the biggest months first
SELECT EXTRACT(MONTH FROM date) AS month_num,
       COUNT(unique_key)        AS mv_thefts
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
WHERE year = 2016
  AND primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
GROUP BY month_num
ORDER BY mv_thefts DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Grab only the single month with the highest count
SELECT EXTRACT(MONTH FROM date) AS month_num,
       COUNT(unique_key)        AS mv_thefts
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
WHERE year = 2016
  AND primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
GROUP BY month_num
ORDER BY mv_thefts DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

```sql
-- 9. Produce the highest count value without the month label (sub‑query style)
SELECT MAX(mv_thefts) AS max_monthly_mv_thefts_2016
FROM (
  SELECT EXTRACT(MONTH FROM date) AS month_num,
         COUNT(unique_key)        AS mv_thefts
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
  WHERE year = 2016
    AND primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
  GROUP BY month_num
);
```

```sql
-- 10. Same as #9, shown again for clarity (single row / single column answer)
SELECT MAX(monthly_total) AS highest_motor_vehicle_thefts_in_a_2016_month
FROM (
  SELECT EXTRACT(MONTH FROM date) AS month,
         COUNT(unique_key)        AS monthly_total
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
  WHERE year = 2016
    AND primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
  GROUP BY month
);
```

